": "hint", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-03-06T07:04:03Z", "username": "Pavan Pamidimarri", "text": "Stalin", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-03-06T07:04:05Z", "username": "Abhishek Vats", "text": "6. The meeting between Mahatma Gandhi and X, took place in December 1935 at Gandhi’s ashram in Wardha when X had traveled to India specifically to win Gandhi’s endorsement for artificial contraception, believing his support would revolutionize women's health in Asia.\nX approached the issue from a humanitarian and feminist perspective. She argued that women were being \"broken\" by frequent childbirth and that contraception was a tool for female liberation and poverty reduction. She famously told him, \"The woman has the right to control her own body.”\nGandhi argued that using contraceptives would lead to \"moral bankruptcy\" and turn men and women into \"animals.\" He insisted that the only \"moral\" way to limit a family was through Satyagraha—total self-restraint and abstinence", "role": "hint", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-03-06T07:04:52Z", "username": "Dr.Namrata Srinivasan", "text": "Clue", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-03-06T07:04:55Z", "username": "Pavan Pamidimarri", "text": "sanger", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-03-06T07:04:58Z", "username": "Pavan Pamidimarri", "text": "margaret", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-03-06T07:05:06Z", "username": "Pavan Pamidimarri", "text": "the pioneer of the pill", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}]